1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,839
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 1.

2
00:00:02,839 --> 00:00:06,151
Angular Signals: State Ownership and Component Contracts.

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,231
Learning goals and the review method

4
00:00:11,356 --> 00:00:13,850
Welcome to this instructional lesson on Angular signals

5
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:16,344
for senior engineers building a shared component library.

6
00:00:16,344 --> 00:00:18,839
We will first build a working mental model,

7
00:00:18,839 --> 00:00:22,892
then review four real scenarios from this repository: a chip selection that drifts,

8
00:00:22,892 --> 00:00:26,010
a switch that ignores its parent, effects that leak resources,

9
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:28,193
and a table that mutates consumer data.

10
00:00:28,193 --> 00:00:30,999
You will see the faulty code, hear the question,

11
00:00:30,999 --> 00:00:34,429
have time to think, and compare your answer with a detailed

12
00:00:34,429 --> 00:00:34,740
explanation.

13
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:36,923
We will also answer every follow-up question.

14
00:00:36,923 --> 00:00:40,665
Pause the video whenever you want more time to inspect the code.

15
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:44,507
Learning goals and the review method · 2

16
00:00:45,633 --> 00:00:48,408
These questions are not mainly about remembering function names.

17
00:00:48,408 --> 00:00:51,493
They test whether you can identify who owns a value,

18
00:00:51,493 --> 00:00:54,885
what invalidates a derivation, when a resource is acquired and released,

19
00:00:54,885 --> 00:00:58,586
and how a component behaves when another team uses it differently from

20
00:00:58,586 --> 00:00:59,512
your own application.

21
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:02,904
A senior answer connects the reported symptom to a precise mechanism.

22
00:01:02,904 --> 00:01:05,989
It then proposes a correction, identifies a test that would

23
00:01:05,989 --> 00:01:08,765
catch the defect, and explains the effect on consumers.

24
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:13,170
Learning goals and the review method · 3

25
00:01:14,295 --> 00:01:16,567
Use four steps in each review.

26
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:21,869
First, trace the state from the parent through the input to the rendered template.

27
00:01:21,869 --> 00:01:25,657
Second, identify every write and the event that causes it.

28
00:01:25,657 --> 00:01:29,823
Third, distinguish derived values from values that users can independently change.

29
00:01:29,823 --> 00:01:33,611
Fourth, examine the public contract: inputs, outputs, accessibility, and compatibility.

30
00:01:33,611 --> 00:01:38,534
This method works beyond signals, but signals make the dependency graph unusually visible.

31
00:01:38,534 --> 00:01:42,322
Our examples use the Angular version installed in this repository.

32
00:01:42,322 --> 00:01:47,624
Version-dependent behavior will be identified rather than assumed to apply to every older release.

33
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,804
Writable signals and tracked reads

34
00:01:51,929 --> 00:01:55,536
A signal is a value container with a getter function.

35
00:01:55,536 --> 00:01:58,062
Calling the getter reads the current value.

36
00:01:58,062 --> 00:02:02,391
In a reactive context, Angular also records that read as a dependency.

37
00:02:02,391 --> 00:02:07,442
The context matters: a computed derivation, an effect, and a template establish reactive dependencies.

38
00:02:07,442 --> 00:02:11,411
Reading a signal once in an ordinary constructor field initializer does

39
00:02:11,411 --> 00:02:15,018
not turn the resulting plain value into a reactive relationship.

40
00:02:15,018 --> 00:02:17,905
That distinction explains several bugs we will encounter.

41
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:21,457
Writable signals and tracked reads · 2

42
00:02:22,582 --> 00:02:24,136
Look at the counter example.

43
00:02:24,136 --> 00:02:27,246
We create a count with an initial value of zero.

44
00:02:27,246 --> 00:02:28,490
Calling count gives zero.

45
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:30,667
Setting it to three replaces the value.

46
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:33,154
Updating it with a function that adds one

47
00:02:33,154 --> 00:02:35,642
derives a new value from the current value.

48
00:02:35,642 --> 00:02:39,996
Set is useful when the new value already exists, such as a server response.

49
00:02:39,996 --> 00:02:43,416
Update is useful when the change depends on the current state.

50
00:02:43,416 --> 00:02:47,148
Neither operation is a promise to notify consumers regardless of the result.

51
00:02:47,148 --> 00:02:49,325
Equality still determines whether a change occurred.

52
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,972
Writable signals and tracked reads · 3

53
00:02:54,098 --> 00:02:56,396
The template reads count by calling it.

54
00:02:56,396 --> 00:03:00,007
It does not receive a snapshot that we must manually refresh.

55
00:03:00,007 --> 00:03:02,962
Angular knows that this view depends on the signal.

56
00:03:02,962 --> 00:03:05,916
That relationship also works with OnPush components: signal changes

57
00:03:05,916 --> 00:03:08,871
can mark a consuming component for a subsequent update.

58
00:03:08,871 --> 00:03:11,826
This is not a guarantee that every arbitrary mutable

59
00:03:11,826 --> 00:03:14,452
object, asynchronous callback, or property change becomes reactive.

60
00:03:14,452 --> 00:03:17,407
Ask which signal changed and which consumer read it.

61
00:03:17,407 --> 00:03:19,705
If you cannot point to that connection,

62
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:22,988
the word reactive is hiding an assumption rather than explaining

63
00:03:22,988 --> 00:03:23,645
the behavior.

64
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:26,741
Equality and immutable collection updates

65
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:31,307
Signals use referential equality by default, through Object dot is.

66
00:03:31,307 --> 00:03:33,717
For primitive values that is usually intuitive.

67
00:03:33,717 --> 00:03:36,470
Setting false to false does not notify consumers.

68
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:41,289
With an array, however, the reference can stay the same while its contents change.

69
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:44,387
Pushing into an existing array and returning that array

70
00:03:44,387 --> 00:03:47,141
from update does not create a different reference.

71
00:03:47,141 --> 00:03:50,583
A computed that previously read the array can retain a

72
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:54,025
cached result because the signal did not report a change.

73
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,698
Equality and immutable collection updates · 2

74
00:03:58,823 --> 00:04:01,120
The two collection examples show the difference.

75
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:05,716
The incorrect version pushes a value into the list and returns the same list.

76
00:04:05,716 --> 00:04:08,342
The corrected version returns a fresh array containing

77
00:04:08,342 --> 00:04:10,639
the previous elements and the new element.

78
00:04:10,639 --> 00:04:13,922
Removing an item with filter also produces a fresh array.

79
00:04:13,922 --> 00:04:16,876
The point is not that the syntax is fashionable.

80
00:04:16,876 --> 00:04:19,830
The new reference is the observable state transition that

81
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:22,785
tells dependent code its cached result may be invalid.

82
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,678
Equality and immutable collection updates · 3

83
00:04:27,803 --> 00:04:30,024
Immutability also defines an ownership boundary.

84
00:04:30,024 --> 00:04:34,098
A component receiving an array should not sort it in place,

85
00:04:34,098 --> 00:04:38,171
append to it, or change consumer-owned records while calculating a view.

86
00:04:38,171 --> 00:04:42,615
Otherwise a table can unexpectedly change an export elsewhere in the application.

87
00:04:42,615 --> 00:04:46,318
A readonly array type helps the compiler reject common mutations.

88
00:04:46,318 --> 00:04:49,281
It is not a runtime freeze and does

89
00:04:49,281 --> 00:04:51,873
not deeply freeze objects inside the array.

90
00:04:51,873 --> 00:04:55,206
If you hand an internal array to untrusted JavaScript,

91
00:04:55,206 --> 00:04:58,909
a type annotation cannot stop that caller from mutating it.

92
00:04:58,909 --> 00:05:02,242
Copies, stable immutable contracts, or deliberate defensive freezing may

93
00:05:02,242 --> 00:05:05,205
be appropriate depending on the API and cost.

94
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:08,231
Computed values are cached derivations

95
00:05:09,357 --> 00:05:13,651
A computed is a read-only signal whose value is derived from other signals.

96
00:05:13,651 --> 00:05:17,616
Its computation is lazy: it runs when a consumer first reads it.

97
00:05:17,616 --> 00:05:20,259
Its result is memoized until a dependency changes.

98
00:05:20,259 --> 00:05:23,893
Reading the computed repeatedly without invalidation should reuse the cached result.

99
00:05:23,893 --> 00:05:27,527
This is why a computed is often a good place for

100
00:05:27,527 --> 00:05:29,839
a filtered list or a selected-value lookup,

101
00:05:29,839 --> 00:05:33,473
rather than an ordinary template method that repeats the work whenever

102
00:05:33,473 --> 00:05:34,464
it is called.

103
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:38,323
Computed values are cached derivations · 2

104
00:05:39,448 --> 00:05:43,350
The total example reads quantity and unit price, then multiplies them.

105
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:46,898
There is no reason to create a separate writable total

106
00:05:46,898 --> 00:05:50,445
and an effect that copies the multiplication result into it.

107
00:05:50,445 --> 00:05:52,929
Total is determined by two existing facts.

108
00:05:52,929 --> 00:05:57,895
Maintaining an extra stored total creates another write path and another opportunity for disagreement.

109
00:05:57,895 --> 00:06:01,088
A pure computed says directly what the value means.

110
00:06:01,088 --> 00:06:03,926
It also makes a useful test easy: change

111
00:06:03,926 --> 00:06:06,765
quantity, read total, and assert the expected result.

112
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:10,354
Computed values are cached derivations · 3

113
00:06:11,479 --> 00:06:12,744
Keep a derivation pure.

114
00:06:12,744 --> 00:06:15,591
Do not emit an output, mutate an input array,

115
00:06:15,591 --> 00:06:18,438
or register an event listener while evaluating a computed.

116
00:06:18,438 --> 00:06:21,917
Consumers can read the result at times you do not control,

117
00:06:21,917 --> 00:06:25,081
and memoization means the computation is not an event handler.

118
00:06:25,081 --> 00:06:27,295
A derivation may allocate a new result,

119
00:06:27,295 --> 00:06:30,458
but it should not change the facts from which it

120
00:06:30,458 --> 00:06:31,407
derives that result.

121
00:06:31,407 --> 00:06:35,520
When a table sorts a copy of its input, that is a derivation.

122
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:37,418
When it sorts the input itself,

123
00:06:37,418 --> 00:06:39,948
it is an unexpected write disguised as a

124
00:06:39,948 --> 00:06:40,264
calculation.

125
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:43,372
Dynamic dependencies and narrow derivations

126
00:06:44,497 --> 00:06:48,305
Dependencies are determined by the signals actually read during the latest

127
00:06:48,305 --> 00:06:52,114
execution, not by all the signals mentioned in the source code.

128
00:06:52,114 --> 00:06:54,885
In the conditional example, the detailed view reads

129
00:06:54,885 --> 00:06:57,309
the price only when showDetails is true.

130
00:06:57,309 --> 00:07:01,810
When it is false, the computation returns a simple label without reading price.

131
00:07:01,810 --> 00:07:05,273
A price change therefore does not invalidate that result through

132
00:07:05,273 --> 00:07:08,389
a dependency that was never established in that branch.

133
00:07:08,389 --> 00:07:12,545
When showDetails changes, the computation can establish a different set of dependencies.

134
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:16,557
Dynamic dependencies and narrow derivations · 2

135
00:07:17,683 --> 00:07:19,943
This behavior makes narrow computeds useful.

136
00:07:19,943 --> 00:07:24,840
Imagine one computed that returns an object containing sorted rows and a count.

137
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:30,115
Changing the sort may allocate a new wrapper even though the count is unchanged.

138
00:07:30,115 --> 00:07:34,636
Consumers interested only in the count now depend on the wrapper's identity.

139
00:07:34,636 --> 00:07:38,780
Separate sortedRows and rowCount computeds communicate the independent meanings more clearly.

140
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:41,793
A custom equality function can suppress equivalent results,

141
00:07:41,793 --> 00:07:46,314
but it should express a real equivalence rule rather than conceal mutations

142
00:07:46,314 --> 00:07:47,445
or stale data.

143
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:51,344
Dynamic dependencies and narrow derivations · 3

144
00:07:52,469 --> 00:07:56,075
Do not assume that equality prevents the expensive computation itself.

145
00:07:56,075 --> 00:07:59,321
Angular must obtain the new computed result before it

146
00:07:59,321 --> 00:08:02,206
can compare that result with the old one.

147
00:08:02,206 --> 00:08:05,451
If sorting thousands of records is the costly part,

148
00:08:05,451 --> 00:08:08,697
a result equality function does not eliminate the sort.

149
00:08:08,697 --> 00:08:11,222
First stabilize inputs and measure the cost.

150
00:08:11,222 --> 00:08:15,189
Then consider narrow dependencies, sensible caching, or a different data strategy.

151
00:08:15,189 --> 00:08:18,795
Deep comparison also has a cost, and an equality function

152
00:08:18,795 --> 00:08:22,401
that ignores a relevant field can leave the view stale.

153
00:08:22,401 --> 00:08:24,205
Correctness comes before suppressing notifications.

154
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:27,066
Inputs and construction order

155
00:08:28,191 --> 00:08:31,906
An input signal represents data supplied by Angular's binding system.

156
00:08:31,906 --> 00:08:34,135
Input declarations belong in component initialization,

157
00:08:34,135 --> 00:08:37,107
but consuming the eventual bound value during construction

158
00:08:37,107 --> 00:08:37,850
is different.

159
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:40,822
Field initializers and constructors run before Angular has

160
00:08:40,822 --> 00:08:43,422
assigned the inputs for the first render.

161
00:08:43,422 --> 00:08:47,509
A non-required input read at that moment returns its declared default.

162
00:08:47,509 --> 00:08:50,852
A required input read before assignment can fail because

163
00:08:50,852 --> 00:08:53,824
there is not yet a value to read.

164
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:57,298
Inputs and construction order · 2

165
00:08:58,423 --> 00:09:00,521
Suppose options has an empty-array default.

166
00:09:00,521 --> 00:09:04,019
If a selected field is initialized by immediately filtering options,

167
00:09:04,019 --> 00:09:08,216
selected starts empty even when the parent is about to supply selected

168
00:09:08,216 --> 00:09:08,566
options.

169
00:09:08,566 --> 00:09:11,014
Angular will later update the input signal,

170
00:09:11,014 --> 00:09:14,512
but selected is an independent writable signal created from an

171
00:09:14,512 --> 00:09:15,211
earlier snapshot.

172
00:09:15,211 --> 00:09:18,359
Nothing in that field initializer declares an ongoing dependency.

173
00:09:18,359 --> 00:09:21,857
Moving the snapshot to the first lifecycle callback can fix

174
00:09:21,857 --> 00:09:25,004
the first assignment while still leaving later changes unsynchronized.

175
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:28,283
Inputs and construction order · 3

176
00:09:29,408 --> 00:09:32,613
The correct design depends on the meaning of the value.

177
00:09:32,613 --> 00:09:35,819
If selection is entirely determined by options, use a computed.

178
00:09:35,819 --> 00:09:38,705
If the user can edit selection but changes to

179
00:09:38,705 --> 00:09:41,269
options must reset or reconcile it, consider linkedSignal.

180
00:09:41,269 --> 00:09:43,193
If the parent owns the selection,

181
00:09:43,193 --> 00:09:45,758
make that ownership explicit in the input and

182
00:09:45,758 --> 00:09:46,399
output contract.

183
00:09:46,399 --> 00:09:50,246
Never pick a lifecycle hook as a substitute for defining state ownership.

184
00:09:50,246 --> 00:09:52,811
In a review, say what happens before binding,

185
00:09:52,811 --> 00:09:55,055
after the first binding, after user interaction,

186
00:09:55,055 --> 00:09:56,979
and after a later parent update.

187
00:09:56,979 --> 00:10:00,184
Those four moments reveal whether your design is actually complete.

188
00:10:01,559 --> 00:10:03,444
Linked signals for writable dependent state

189
00:10:04,569 --> 00:10:07,080
A linked signal combines writable state with a

190
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,592
dependency that determines when it should be recomputed.

191
00:10:09,592 --> 00:10:12,418
Unlike a computed, the user can change its value.

192
00:10:12,418 --> 00:10:15,243
Unlike an ordinary independent signal, it also knows that

193
00:10:15,243 --> 00:10:18,069
a source change affects the validity of that value.

194
00:10:18,069 --> 00:10:21,837
This is useful when a selected shipping method depends on available methods,

195
00:10:21,837 --> 00:10:25,604
or a selected chip list depends on the options that still exist.

196
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:29,920
Linked signals for writable dependent state · 2

197
00:10:31,045 --> 00:10:34,352
The source and computation form lets you access both

198
00:10:34,352 --> 00:10:37,291
the new source and the previous linked value.

199
00:10:37,291 --> 00:10:40,966
On the first computation, initialize from the options marked selected.

200
00:10:40,966 --> 00:10:43,906
On subsequent option changes, retain the user's previous

201
00:10:43,906 --> 00:10:46,478
selections only where their identifiers still exist.

202
00:10:46,478 --> 00:10:47,581
This distinction matters.

203
00:10:47,581 --> 00:10:52,725
Reinitializing from selected flags on every new options array would erase the user's edits.

204
00:10:52,725 --> 00:10:56,400
Preserving everything forever would leave selections referring to deleted options.

205
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,605
Reconciliation states the exact middle ground.

206
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,830
Linked signals for writable dependent state · 3

207
00:11:03,955 --> 00:11:06,805
The source must itself communicate changes through signals.

208
00:11:06,805 --> 00:11:09,655
Mutating an options array in place without publishing

209
00:11:09,655 --> 00:11:12,505
a new value does not reliably trigger reconciliation.

210
00:11:12,505 --> 00:11:15,712
Conversely, a parent that creates a fresh but equivalent

211
00:11:15,712 --> 00:11:18,562
array on every check can cause unnecessary recomputation.

212
00:11:18,562 --> 00:11:22,125
Recommend a stable signal or computed on the consumer side.

213
00:11:22,125 --> 00:11:25,331
If you add equality, compare all fields relevant to

214
00:11:25,331 --> 00:11:28,181
the contract, not only labels or array length.

215
00:11:28,181 --> 00:11:31,032
Also decide whether automatic pruning produces an output.

216
00:11:31,032 --> 00:11:34,238
A user-action event and an effective-state event are different

217
00:11:34,238 --> 00:11:37,444
promises, and a shared library should not blur them.

218
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:40,566
Models and two-way component binding

219
00:11:41,691 --> 00:11:45,946
A model input is a writable signal exposed as a component input.

220
00:11:45,946 --> 00:11:48,428
Angular also creates a corresponding change output.

221
00:11:48,428 --> 00:11:51,265
A checked model creates checkedChange, allowing a parent

222
00:11:51,265 --> 00:11:53,747
to use the conventional two-way binding syntax.

223
00:11:53,747 --> 00:11:57,293
The component can update checked directly; it does not need

224
00:11:57,293 --> 00:12:00,484
a second local signal plus a manual synchronization effect.

225
00:12:00,484 --> 00:12:04,385
A parent change updates the same value that the template reads.

226
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:08,015
Models and two-way component binding · 2

227
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:13,105
This solves the ordinary reset problem: the parent turns a setting off

228
00:12:13,105 --> 00:12:16,740
and the switch renders off because both use one model value.

229
00:12:16,740 --> 00:12:20,045
It also supports a component used without a parent binding,

230
00:12:20,045 --> 00:12:23,349
where the model's default and user updates provide local behavior.

231
00:12:23,349 --> 00:12:27,314
Those are useful capabilities, but the API still needs a documented contract.

232
00:12:27,314 --> 00:12:29,628
Does the component commit a change immediately,

233
00:12:29,628 --> 00:12:32,271
or does it merely request permission from the

234
00:12:32,271 --> 00:12:32,602
parent?

235
00:12:32,602 --> 00:12:34,585
A model naturally performs the former.

236
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:38,468
Models and two-way component binding · 3

237
00:12:39,593 --> 00:12:41,742
A rejected change exposes the difference.

238
00:12:41,742 --> 00:12:46,041
If the model flips to true while the parent keeps its one-way

239
00:12:46,041 --> 00:12:50,339
bound false unchanged, Angular has no changed parent value to write back.

240
00:12:50,339 --> 00:12:52,131
The switch can remain on.

241
00:12:52,131 --> 00:12:55,355
Even an immediate two-way change-and-revert can collapse to the

242
00:12:55,355 --> 00:12:58,579
same previously bound value before Angular processes the input.

243
00:12:58,579 --> 00:13:02,161
Do not promise that any synchronous rollback automatically repairs this.

244
00:13:02,161 --> 00:13:04,669
For strict parent authority or asynchronous approval,

245
00:13:04,669 --> 00:13:08,609
use a read-only input plus a request output and render only

246
00:13:08,609 --> 00:13:10,042
the parent's accepted state.

247
00:13:10,042 --> 00:13:13,625
We will show that alternative explicitly during the switch walkthrough.

248
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,761
Choosing a controlled component contract

249
00:13:17,886 --> 00:13:21,410
In the controlled example, the switch reads checked from an

250
00:13:21,410 --> 00:13:24,934
input and emits a requested value from a separate output.

251
00:13:24,934 --> 00:13:27,753
Clicking does not change an internal checked signal.

252
00:13:27,753 --> 00:13:31,629
If the parent accepts, it updates checked and the view changes.

253
00:13:31,629 --> 00:13:35,857
If the parent rejects, checked remains unchanged and the view remains unchanged.

254
00:13:35,857 --> 00:13:38,676
There is no speculative local commit to undo.

255
00:13:38,676 --> 00:13:42,905
This can be particularly useful for authorization, confirmation dialogs, or server-validated settings.

256
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:46,737
Choosing a controlled component contract · 2

257
00:13:47,863 --> 00:13:50,745
The tradeoff is that an unbound controlled component

258
00:13:50,745 --> 00:13:53,627
does not automatically maintain a useful local value.

259
00:13:53,627 --> 00:13:56,870
If you need both behaviors, distinguish them explicitly through

260
00:13:56,870 --> 00:13:59,752
an API that consumers can understand and test.

261
00:13:59,752 --> 00:14:02,274
Avoid silently combining an input, a model,

262
00:14:02,274 --> 00:14:05,516
and a second local signal while hoping synchronization will

263
00:14:05,516 --> 00:14:06,237
settle conflicts.

264
00:14:06,237 --> 00:14:08,039
Also choose output names carefully.

265
00:14:08,039 --> 00:14:12,722
A request event says an interaction occurred, not that the effective state changed.

266
00:14:12,722 --> 00:14:15,604
A change event normally implies a committed change.

267
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:19,480
Choosing a controlled component contract · 3

268
00:14:20,605 --> 00:14:22,815
Boolean syntax, disabled behavior, accessible names,

269
00:14:22,815 --> 00:14:25,761
and forms integration are part of the same

270
00:14:25,761 --> 00:14:26,130
contract.

271
00:14:26,130 --> 00:14:29,813
A model does not accept an input transform like booleanAttribute.

272
00:14:29,813 --> 00:14:32,391
Document property binding for its boolean value.

273
00:14:32,391 --> 00:14:35,706
A controlled input can use that transform when appropriate.

274
00:14:35,706 --> 00:14:38,653
Native button disabled behavior is usually preferable to

275
00:14:38,653 --> 00:14:41,231
ignoring clicks in an event handler alone.

276
00:14:41,231 --> 00:14:45,283
Reactive forms require a clear ControlValueAccessor relationship; they should not create

277
00:14:45,283 --> 00:14:48,966
a second hidden value that fights with the signal model.

278
00:14:48,966 --> 00:14:52,281
These concerns are not extras after the reactivity fix.

279
00:14:52,281 --> 00:14:55,965
They are what makes the fix usable across product teams.

280
00:14:57,340 --> 00:14:59,306
Effects connect state to external systems

281
00:15:00,431 --> 00:15:04,470
Use an effect when reactive state must synchronize with a non-reactive system,

282
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:07,500
such as a browser storage API, a charting library,

283
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,183
or an imperative event registration.

284
00:15:09,183 --> 00:15:12,549
It tracks signal reads and reruns when those dependencies change.

285
00:15:12,549 --> 00:15:15,915
That is different from calculating another piece of application state.

286
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:20,628
For a value determined by signals, start with a computed or a linked signal

287
00:15:20,628 --> 00:15:25,005
and only use an effect when an external side effect is genuinely required.

288
00:15:26,380 --> 00:15:29,242
Effects connect state to external systems · 2

289
00:15:30,367 --> 00:15:32,513
Consider the self-triggering pattern on screen.

290
00:15:32,513 --> 00:15:37,522
The effect reads selected and writes the result of filtering selected back into selected.

291
00:15:37,522 --> 00:15:41,457
Filter creates a fresh array even if the contents are identical.

292
00:15:41,457 --> 00:15:46,108
The write changes a dependency the effect has just read, scheduling another run.

293
00:15:46,108 --> 00:15:48,254
Each run writes another fresh reference.

294
00:15:48,254 --> 00:15:53,263
The problem is a feedback loop in the dependency graph, not a missing timeout.

295
00:15:53,263 --> 00:15:56,840
Adding a delay merely changes how fast the loop repeats.

296
00:15:56,840 --> 00:15:59,702
Using untracked to hide a dependency can remove

297
00:15:59,702 --> 00:16:02,565
one trigger while leaving an unclear synchronization design.

298
00:16:03,940 --> 00:16:06,707
Effects connect state to external systems · 3

299
00:16:07,832 --> 00:16:09,603
Effects also have lifecycle requirements.

300
00:16:09,603 --> 00:16:14,563
When a new run replaces an external resource, the old resource must be released.

301
00:16:14,563 --> 00:16:18,106
If an effect installs a document listener every time a

302
00:16:18,106 --> 00:16:21,295
popover opens, closing the popover should release that listener.

303
00:16:21,295 --> 00:16:23,775
Destroying the component must release it too.

304
00:16:23,775 --> 00:16:27,673
A correct answer identifies both the resource and its intended lifetime.

305
00:16:27,673 --> 00:16:31,216
It does not merely say that Angular cleans up effects,

306
00:16:31,216 --> 00:16:35,822
because Angular cannot infer how to undo an arbitrary browser API call inside

307
00:16:35,822 --> 00:16:36,884
an effect callback.

308
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:40,491
Cleanup is part of resource ownership

309
00:16:41,616 --> 00:16:44,589
An effect callback receives a cleanup registration function.

310
00:16:44,589 --> 00:16:47,935
Register the action that undoes what this run created.

311
00:16:47,935 --> 00:16:53,139
Angular invokes that cleanup before the next run and when the effect is destroyed.

312
00:16:53,139 --> 00:16:56,485
A normal component-scoped effect is destroyed with its owning

313
00:16:56,485 --> 00:16:59,459
context unless you deliberately opt into another lifecycle.

314
00:16:59,459 --> 00:17:02,805
Resource cleanup inside the effect is still your responsibility.

315
00:17:04,180 --> 00:17:06,861
Cleanup is part of resource ownership · 2

316
00:17:07,986 --> 00:17:11,671
The listener example uses one named handler for addition and removal.

317
00:17:11,671 --> 00:17:15,357
The same event type, function identity, and capture setting must match.

318
00:17:15,357 --> 00:17:18,372
Creating a second anonymous function that looks identical is

319
00:17:18,372 --> 00:17:21,053
not enough: it is a different function object.

320
00:17:21,053 --> 00:17:24,403
Registering removal only in the component's destruction hook may release

321
00:17:24,403 --> 00:17:27,754
the last listener at the end, but it does not

322
00:17:27,754 --> 00:17:31,105
manage repeated open-close cycles if every opening created another handler.

323
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:35,423
Cleanup is part of resource ownership · 3

324
00:17:36,548 --> 00:17:39,565
A one-time listener is not the same lifetime.

325
00:17:39,565 --> 00:17:42,960
With once enabled, the first keydown event removes the

326
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:45,977
listener, even if that key was not Escape.

327
00:17:45,977 --> 00:17:50,503
A user pressing Tab before Escape would disable the desired close behavior.

328
00:17:50,503 --> 00:17:53,898
This illustrates a general review technique: reason about events

329
00:17:53,898 --> 00:17:56,915
that do not satisfy the handler's inner condition.

330
00:17:56,915 --> 00:18:01,818
For subscriptions, timers, observers, and listeners, describe acquisition, repeated use, replacement, and destruction.

331
00:18:01,818 --> 00:18:06,345
That lifecycle explanation is more valuable than memorizing a cleanup API name.

332
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:09,688
DOM work after rendering

333
00:18:10,813 --> 00:18:13,686
Ordinary effects are not a general guarantee that

334
00:18:13,686 --> 00:18:16,560
the final browser layout is ready to measure.

335
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:19,793
DOM placement may depend on elements created by a

336
00:18:19,793 --> 00:18:23,027
conditional template, changes in other components, styles, and layout.

337
00:18:23,027 --> 00:18:27,697
When work specifically depends on rendered DOM, afterRenderEffect provides a post-render integration point.

338
00:18:27,697 --> 00:18:30,571
Its callbacks do not run during server-side rendering,

339
00:18:30,571 --> 00:18:33,444
where there is no browser layout to inspect.

340
00:18:34,820 --> 00:18:37,702
DOM work after rendering · 2

341
00:18:38,827 --> 00:18:41,081
Separate reading layout from writing styles.

342
00:18:41,081 --> 00:18:44,086
The example reads an anchor rectangle in earlyRead,

343
00:18:44,086 --> 00:18:47,843
then passes the measurement to a write phase that positions

344
00:18:47,843 --> 00:18:48,594
the panel.

345
00:18:48,594 --> 00:18:51,600
The phase argument is exposed as a signal,

346
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:54,981
so the write callback reads that measurement through its

347
00:18:54,981 --> 00:18:55,357
getter.

348
00:18:55,357 --> 00:18:59,489
Batching reads and writes across components helps reduce unnecessary layout recalculation.

349
00:18:59,489 --> 00:19:02,495
Mixing getBoundingClientRect with immediate style changes in many

350
00:19:02,495 --> 00:19:05,124
independent callbacks can force repeated layout work.

351
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:09,465
DOM work after rendering · 3

352
00:19:10,590 --> 00:19:13,556
This is still only a minimal positioning example.

353
00:19:13,556 --> 00:19:18,745
Scrolling, resizing, clipping containers, viewport edges, stacked overlays, and focus restoration require more design.

354
00:19:18,745 --> 00:19:23,193
A shared library should usually build on an established positioning and overlay

355
00:19:23,193 --> 00:19:27,642
system rather than ship a few coordinate assignments as a complete solution.

356
00:19:27,642 --> 00:19:31,719
Rendering at the right time fixes one class of defect; it

357
00:19:31,719 --> 00:19:35,426
does not establish collision handling, layer ownership, or accessible semantics.

358
00:19:35,426 --> 00:19:40,245
Separate the demonstrated correction from what you would require before a production release.

359
00:19:41,619 --> 00:19:43,681
Row identity and accessible derived views

360
00:19:44,807 --> 00:19:46,928
Signals do not replace DOM identity.

361
00:19:46,928 --> 00:19:51,878
When a list is sorted, tracking by index associates a view with a position.

362
00:19:51,878 --> 00:19:54,707
The record occupying that position can change while

363
00:19:54,707 --> 00:19:57,182
local state stays with the reused view.

364
00:19:57,182 --> 00:20:02,133
A focused checkbox or expanded row may now appear attached to a different record.

365
00:20:02,133 --> 00:20:05,669
A stable unique record identifier tells Angular which views correspond

366
00:20:05,669 --> 00:20:09,205
to the same logical rows even when their positions move.

367
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:13,549
Row identity and accessible derived views · 2

368
00:20:14,674 --> 00:20:17,643
Stable tracking and stable inputs solve different problems.

369
00:20:17,643 --> 00:20:21,354
Tracking can preserve views across a new array of records,

370
00:20:21,354 --> 00:20:25,437
but it does not automatically eliminate sorting work or all binding

371
00:20:25,437 --> 00:20:25,808
updates.

372
00:20:25,808 --> 00:20:29,148
A stable computed in the parent can avoid producing

373
00:20:29,148 --> 00:20:32,117
an equivalent filtered array on every unrelated check.

374
00:20:32,117 --> 00:20:35,829
A column-specific comparator can correctly handle dates or numeric data.

375
00:20:35,829 --> 00:20:37,684
Sorting a copy preserves ownership.

376
00:20:37,684 --> 00:20:41,024
These are complementary decisions, not one universal performance switch.

377
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:45,301
Row identity and accessible derived views · 3

378
00:20:46,426 --> 00:20:48,239
Accessibility also changes the implementation.

379
00:20:48,239 --> 00:20:52,953
A click handler on a table header does not provide a keyboard-operable control.

380
00:20:52,953 --> 00:20:55,491
Use a real button in the header.

381
00:20:55,491 --> 00:20:59,118
Put aria sort on the sorted header to expose direction,

382
00:20:59,118 --> 00:21:03,107
and decide whether a live announcement helps users understand the resulting

383
00:21:03,107 --> 00:21:03,470
change.

384
00:21:03,470 --> 00:21:07,459
A chip toggle uses pressed state; a switch uses checked state.

385
00:21:07,459 --> 00:21:11,448
Match semantics to the interaction rather than copying attributes between components.

386
00:21:11,448 --> 00:21:16,524
Readability, state correctness, keyboard operation, and a stable public contract should be verified together.

387
00:21:17,900 --> 00:21:19,705
How to prove the correction

388
00:21:20,830 --> 00:21:25,161
Before we begin the questions, turn each claim into a concrete observation.

389
00:21:25,161 --> 00:21:28,410
For immutable selection updates, inspect both the selected values

390
00:21:28,410 --> 00:21:31,298
and the rendered pressed state after a click.

391
00:21:31,298 --> 00:21:34,547
For linked selection, remove an option and confirm that

392
00:21:34,547 --> 00:21:37,435
invalid selections disappear while valid user choices remain.

393
00:21:37,435 --> 00:21:40,323
For a parent reset, change the bound value

394
00:21:40,323 --> 00:21:43,211
and confirm that the displayed switch follows it.

395
00:21:43,211 --> 00:21:48,265
For rejection, deliberately keep the parent's accepted value unchanged and verify the controlled contract.

396
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:51,857
How to prove the correction · 2

397
00:21:52,982 --> 00:21:55,902
For cleanup, open and close the popover repeatedly and

398
00:21:55,902 --> 00:21:58,822
confirm that the active listener count returns to zero.

399
00:21:58,822 --> 00:22:01,418
Destroy it while open and confirm cleanup again.

400
00:22:01,418 --> 00:22:04,338
For positioning, measure the anchor in an actual browser

401
00:22:04,338 --> 00:22:06,934
and compare the panel's assigned coordinates after rendering.

402
00:22:06,934 --> 00:22:10,179
For a table, retain a copy of the original order,

403
00:22:10,179 --> 00:22:13,748
sort the displayed view, and confirm that the consumer's array is

404
00:22:13,748 --> 00:22:14,073
unchanged.

405
00:22:14,073 --> 00:22:16,669
Keep a DOM reference for one record and

406
00:22:16,669 --> 00:22:19,264
confirm it remains that record's row after reordering.

407
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:23,263
How to prove the correction · 3

408
00:22:24,388 --> 00:22:27,760
Compilation is necessary but does not prove these behaviors.

409
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:31,133
Strict template checking catches invalid bindings and missing members,

410
00:22:31,133 --> 00:22:34,505
but it cannot establish event lifetime or browser layout.

411
00:22:34,505 --> 00:22:37,503
A mocked rectangle does not prove that real

412
00:22:37,503 --> 00:22:40,126
layout occurs at the right time either.

413
00:22:40,126 --> 00:22:43,124
Conversely, one working browser demonstration does not prove

414
00:22:43,124 --> 00:22:45,747
every locale, assistive technology, or server-rendering scenario.

415
00:22:45,747 --> 00:22:50,244
State the conditions you actually checked, then explain the remaining production requirements.

416
00:22:50,244 --> 00:22:53,241
That evidence-first habit is the difference between an

417
00:22:53,241 --> 00:22:55,865
attractive answer and a dependable engineering decision.

418
00:22:57,240 --> 00:22:59,227
SIG-001 · Scenario

419
00:23:00,352 --> 00:23:02,587
We now turn to SIG-001.

420
00:23:02,587 --> 00:23:07,505
Inspect state ownership, reactive dependencies, resource lifetimes, and the consumer contract.

421
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:10,647
SIG-001 · Scenario

422
00:23:11,773 --> 00:23:16,623
You maintain @acme/ui, the component library that every Acme product team builds on.

423
00:23:16,623 --> 00:23:21,100
A teammate opens a pull request for a new multi-select chip group.

424
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:25,205
Product teams pass the options in, and some options arrive pre-selected.

425
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:29,403
SIG-001 · Scenario · code 1/4

426
00:23:30,528 --> 00:23:33,705
The chip group renders one native button per option.

427
00:23:35,080 --> 00:23:38,560
SIG-001 · Scenario · code 2/4

428
00:23:39,685 --> 00:23:41,425
Trace the field initializers.

429
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:46,320
SIG-001 · Scenario · code 3/4

430
00:23:47,445 --> 00:23:50,085
Inspect the effect and toggle together.

431
00:23:51,460 --> 00:23:54,443
SIG-001 · Scenario · code 4/4

432
00:23:55,568 --> 00:23:58,925
In the correction, the source is the options input.

433
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:01,985
SIG-001 · Interview question

434
00:24:03,111 --> 00:24:06,505
What problems do you see, and how would you fix each one?

435
00:24:06,505 --> 00:24:07,919
There are at least three.

436
00:24:07,919 --> 00:24:11,313
One of them freezes the page as soon as the component renders.

437
00:24:17,964 --> 00:24:19,121
SIG-001 · 1.

438
00:24:19,121 --> 00:24:23,364
The initial selection is read before the input has a value

439
00:24:24,489 --> 00:24:28,734
Field initializers run inside the constructor, before Angular has set any input.

440
00:24:28,734 --> 00:24:32,979
this.options() returns the default, [], so the initial selection is always empty.

441
00:24:32,979 --> 00:24:37,932
Options the consumer marks selected: true never appear selected, and nothing reports an error.

442
00:24:39,307 --> 00:24:40,292
SIG-001 · 1.

443
00:24:40,292 --> 00:24:43,907
The initial selection is read before the input has a value

444
00:24:45,032 --> 00:24:48,663
If someone later makes the input required to "fix" it,

445
00:24:48,663 --> 00:24:53,384
the same line fails louder: reading a required input before it is set

446
00:24:53,384 --> 00:24:58,105
throws NG0950 at runtime, and recent Angular compilers reject it at build time

447
00:24:58,105 --> 00:24:58,831
with NG8118.

448
00:25:00,207 --> 00:25:01,380
SIG-001 · 1.

449
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:05,684
The initial selection is read before the input has a value

450
00:25:06,809 --> 00:25:09,205
Fix: never read an input during construction.

451
00:25:09,205 --> 00:25:12,972
Derive the initial selection from the input reactively (see issue 3).

452
00:25:14,347 --> 00:25:18,318
SIG-001 · 2. toggle mutates the array, so nothing updates

453
00:25:19,443 --> 00:25:22,288
update returns the same array it was given.

454
00:25:22,288 --> 00:25:26,201
Signals compare values with Object.is, so the signal sees no change

455
00:25:26,201 --> 00:25:30,114
and notifies nobody. selectedSet keeps its cached value, aria-pressed never changes,

456
00:25:30,114 --> 00:25:33,672
and the screen is out of step with the state.

457
00:25:35,047 --> 00:25:38,881
SIG-001 · 2. toggle mutates the array, so nothing updates

458
00:25:40,006 --> 00:25:44,633
There is a second problem in the same lines: selectionChange emits the internal array.

459
00:25:44,633 --> 00:25:47,278
A consumer that sorts or pushes into the

460
00:25:47,278 --> 00:25:49,592
array it receives corrupts the component's state.

461
00:25:50,967 --> 00:25:55,018
SIG-001 · 2. toggle mutates the array, so nothing updates

462
00:25:56,143 --> 00:25:58,624
Fix: return a new array from update,

463
00:25:58,624 --> 00:26:02,168
and type public arrays as readonly to reject mutations in

464
00:26:02,168 --> 00:26:03,232
typed consumer code:

465
00:26:04,607 --> 00:26:08,119
SIG-001 · Answer · code 1/1

466
00:26:09,244 --> 00:26:14,512
The correction preserves valid selections and publishes fresh arrays for user changes.

467
00:26:15,887 --> 00:26:16,977
SIG-001 · 3.

468
00:26:16,977 --> 00:26:18,793
The effect never stops running

469
00:26:19,919 --> 00:26:22,535
The effect reads selected() and writes selected with the

470
00:26:22,535 --> 00:26:25,152
result of filter, which is always a new array.

471
00:26:25,152 --> 00:26:29,222
Writing a new value to a signal the effect depends on schedules the effect

472
00:26:29,222 --> 00:26:33,292
again, so it runs forever and the page freezes as soon as the component

473
00:26:33,292 --> 00:26:37,072
renders. (This is easy to miss in review because each line looks reasonable.)

474
00:26:38,446 --> 00:26:39,634
SIG-001 · 3.

475
00:26:39,634 --> 00:26:41,612
The effect never stops running

476
00:26:42,738 --> 00:26:45,903
Even without the loop, copying one signal into another is

477
00:26:45,903 --> 00:26:48,752
the job of a derived signal, not an effect:

478
00:26:50,127 --> 00:26:51,286
SIG-001 · 3.

479
00:26:51,286 --> 00:26:53,218
The effect never stops running

480
00:26:54,344 --> 00:26:57,746
Effects run on their own schedule, so other code (a computed,

481
00:26:57,746 --> 00:27:01,767
a parent's binding) can briefly read selections that point at options that no

482
00:27:01,767 --> 00:27:02,385
longer exist.

483
00:27:02,385 --> 00:27:05,478
Writing signals inside effects makes the data flow hard to

484
00:27:05,478 --> 00:27:08,572
follow, and loops like this one are easy to create.

485
00:27:09,946 --> 00:27:11,199
SIG-001 · 3.

486
00:27:11,199 --> 00:27:13,287
The effect never stops running

487
00:27:14,412 --> 00:27:15,103
Fix: linkedSignal.

488
00:27:15,103 --> 00:27:19,249
It is writable, like signal, but it recalculates whenever its source changes,

489
00:27:19,249 --> 00:27:24,086
and it receives the previous value so it can keep selections that are still

490
00:27:24,086 --> 00:27:24,432
valid.

491
00:27:25,807 --> 00:27:29,015
SIG-001 · Answer · code 1/4

492
00:27:30,140 --> 00:27:34,952
The correction preserves valid selections and publishes fresh arrays for user changes.

493
00:27:36,327 --> 00:27:39,446
SIG-001 · Answer · code 2/4

494
00:27:40,571 --> 00:27:45,252
The correction preserves valid selections and publishes fresh arrays for user changes.

495
00:27:46,627 --> 00:27:49,986
SIG-001 · Answer · code 3/4

496
00:27:51,112 --> 00:27:56,152
The correction preserves valid selections and publishes fresh arrays for user changes.

497
00:27:57,527 --> 00:28:00,615
SIG-001 · Answer · code 4/4

498
00:28:01,740 --> 00:28:06,372
The correction preserves valid selections and publishes fresh arrays for user changes.

499
00:28:07,747 --> 00:28:11,133
SIG-001 · What a strong candidate also mentions

500
00:28:12,258 --> 00:28:15,426
The group needs an accessible name. role="group" is announced,

501
00:28:15,426 --> 00:28:19,298
but without aria-label or aria-labelledby a screen-reader user hears "group" and

502
00:28:19,298 --> 00:28:20,002
nothing else.

503
00:28:20,002 --> 00:28:22,114
Add an input for the label.

504
00:28:22,114 --> 00:28:25,282
Pruning is a behaviour change consumers must know about.

505
00:28:25,282 --> 00:28:30,211
When options change and a selection disappears, the fixed version does not emit selectionChange.

506
00:28:30,211 --> 00:28:35,139
That is a deliberate choice (outputs report user actions), and it must be documented,

507
00:28:35,139 --> 00:28:39,715
because a consumer storing the selection elsewhere will now hold a stale value.

508
00:28:39,715 --> 00:28:40,419
Controlled use.

509
00:28:40,419 --> 00:28:43,587
Consumers cannot set the selection after the first render.

510
00:28:43,587 --> 00:28:47,812
A model() makes the component usable both controlled and uncontrolled (see SIG-002).

511
00:28:49,187 --> 00:28:51,427
SIG-001 · Runtime boundary

512
00:28:52,552 --> 00:28:57,877
A clarification about the public array: readonly is a compile-time contract, not runtime freezing.

513
00:28:57,877 --> 00:29:01,300
The minimal correction still emits the internal array reference.

514
00:29:01,300 --> 00:29:04,343
A defensive snapshot prevents a consumer from changing

515
00:29:04,343 --> 00:29:07,006
the component state through that emitted array.

516
00:29:07,006 --> 00:29:10,049
Use a copied array or a deliberately documented

517
00:29:10,049 --> 00:29:12,712
immutable contract where runtime isolation is required.

518
00:29:14,087 --> 00:29:15,965
SIG-001 · Follow-up 1

519
00:29:17,090 --> 00:29:20,557
When is an effect the right tool in a library component?

520
00:29:20,557 --> 00:29:21,503
Give one example.

521
00:29:26,954 --> 00:29:31,503
An effect is appropriate when a signal must synchronize with an imperative, non-reactive API.

522
00:29:31,503 --> 00:29:34,427
For example, a chart component may pass a newly

523
00:29:34,427 --> 00:29:37,027
derived data series to a third-party chart instance.

524
00:29:37,027 --> 00:29:40,276
The effect reads the series and calls the chart update

525
00:29:40,276 --> 00:29:43,201
method; cleanup releases any resource created for that run.

526
00:29:43,201 --> 00:29:46,775
It should not calculate the series by writing another application signal.

527
00:29:46,775 --> 00:29:49,375
Use a computed for the series and an

528
00:29:49,375 --> 00:29:51,974
effect only at the boundary to the chart.

529
00:29:51,974 --> 00:29:56,199
If the operation depends on the finished DOM, choose a post-render callback instead.

530
00:29:56,199 --> 00:29:58,473
In an interview, identify the external system,

531
00:29:58,473 --> 00:30:00,748
why template binding cannot do the job,

532
00:30:00,748 --> 00:30:02,698
and how the resource is released.

533
00:30:04,073 --> 00:30:06,244
SIG-001 · Follow-up 2

534
00:30:07,369 --> 00:30:12,826
A consumer passes a new array with the same options on every change detection.

535
00:30:12,826 --> 00:30:17,114
What happens to linkedSignal, and how would you protect against it?

536
00:30:22,301 --> 00:30:27,365
A new reference can invalidate the source, causing the linked computation to reconcile again.

537
00:30:27,365 --> 00:30:30,983
Our fixed chip implementation preserves previous values that remain valid,

538
00:30:30,983 --> 00:30:34,600
so equivalent options do not intentionally reset the user's selection.

539
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:39,665
However, filter still allocates a new selection array, which can produce unnecessary downstream notifications.

540
00:30:39,665 --> 00:30:43,644
First recommend a stable signal or computed to the consumer rather

541
00:30:43,644 --> 00:30:47,262
than calling a method that creates arrays in the template.

542
00:30:47,262 --> 00:30:51,964
Where measurements justify it, return the previous selection reference when reconciliation produces the

543
00:30:51,964 --> 00:30:56,306
same ordered identifiers, or use an equality rule for the linked value.

544
00:30:56,306 --> 00:31:01,008
Do not compare only option count: changed identifiers or selected flags may matter.

545
00:31:01,008 --> 00:31:04,626
Add a test for equivalent replacement options and one for

546
00:31:04,626 --> 00:31:08,244
genuinely removed options, so an optimization cannot hide real changes.

547
00:31:09,619 --> 00:31:11,852
SIG-001 · Follow-up 3

548
00:31:12,977 --> 00:31:16,247
Should selectionChange fire when pruning removes a selection?

549
00:31:16,247 --> 00:31:17,474
Argue both sides.

550
00:31:22,924 --> 00:31:25,928
If selectionChange means a user committed a selection

551
00:31:25,928 --> 00:31:28,932
change, automatic pruning should not emit that event.

552
00:31:28,932 --> 00:31:31,186
The consumer initiated an options change,

553
00:31:31,186 --> 00:31:34,566
and an echoed output could create redundant updates or

554
00:31:34,566 --> 00:31:35,317
feedback loops.

555
00:31:35,317 --> 00:31:39,448
This convention is easy to understand when all outputs represent interactions.

556
00:31:39,448 --> 00:31:43,204
The cost is that a parent storing the selected identifiers

557
00:31:43,204 --> 00:31:46,584
independently can become stale unless it also reconciles them.

558
00:31:46,584 --> 00:31:51,090
If the output means any effective selection change, pruning should be observable.

559
00:31:51,090 --> 00:31:55,221
That keeps dependent application state synchronized, but you must define ordering,

560
00:31:55,221 --> 00:31:59,728
initialization behavior, and how the parent avoids echoing the same state back

561
00:31:59,728 --> 00:32:00,104
indefinitely.

562
00:32:00,104 --> 00:32:03,484
I would document the existing user-action contract and offer

563
00:32:03,484 --> 00:32:06,864
a separate effective-state notification only when consumers need it.

564
00:32:06,864 --> 00:32:10,244
The name, examples, and tests should distinguish the meanings.

565
00:32:11,619 --> 00:32:13,637
SIG-002 · Scenario

566
00:32:14,762 --> 00:32:17,031
We now turn to SIG-002.

567
00:32:17,031 --> 00:32:22,024
Inspect state ownership, reactive dependencies, resource lifetimes, and the consumer contract.

568
00:32:23,399 --> 00:32:24,968
