578
00:33:02,059 --> 00:33:05,504
SIG-002 · Scenario · code 1/1

579
00:33:06,629 --> 00:33:10,504
The template reads isOn, while the parent supplies checked.

580
00:33:11,879 --> 00:33:13,517
SIG-002 · Interview question

581
00:33:14,642 --> 00:33:16,294
Why does the reset not work?

582
00:33:16,294 --> 00:33:18,773
Fix the component, then tell me what other problems

583
00:33:18,773 --> 00:33:21,252
a consumer could run into with your fixed version.

584
00:33:27,902 --> 00:33:29,221
SIG-002 · 1.

585
00:33:29,221 --> 00:33:31,859
Two sources of truth, synchronised once

586
00:33:32,984 --> 00:33:35,464
isOn copies checked once, in ngOnInit.

587
00:33:35,464 --> 00:33:38,357
After that the component ignores its input.

588
00:33:38,357 --> 00:33:42,491
Reset changes settings.emailAlerts, Angular passes the new value to checked,

589
00:33:42,491 --> 00:33:46,212
and nothing reads it: the switch keeps showing isOn.

590
00:33:47,587 --> 00:33:48,734
SIG-002 · 1.

591
00:33:48,734 --> 00:33:51,030
Two sources of truth, synchronised once

592
00:33:52,155 --> 00:33:55,598
The pattern "copy an input into local state" is the root

593
00:33:55,598 --> 00:33:59,041
cause, and it is common in libraries because the component needs

594
00:33:59,041 --> 00:34:02,171
to change the value itself and accept changes from outside.

595
00:34:03,547 --> 00:34:04,657
SIG-002 · 1.

596
00:34:04,657 --> 00:34:06,878
Two sources of truth, synchronised once

597
00:34:08,003 --> 00:34:08,620
Fix: model().

598
00:34:08,620 --> 00:34:12,946
A model is a writable signal that is also an input and an output.

599
00:34:12,946 --> 00:34:15,109
The parent writes it through the binding,

600
00:34:15,109 --> 00:34:17,581
the component writes it with set or update,

601
00:34:17,581 --> 00:34:19,744
and every write from inside emits checkedChange,

602
00:34:19,744 --> 00:34:21,907
so [(checked)] works with no extra code.

603
00:34:21,907 --> 00:34:23,451
There is only one value.

604
00:34:24,827 --> 00:34:28,194
SIG-002 · Answer · code 1/2

605
00:34:29,319 --> 00:34:34,792
The corrected model commits immediately; a controlled input instead waits for parent acceptance.

606
00:34:36,167 --> 00:34:39,442
SIG-002 · Answer · code 2/2

607
00:34:40,567 --> 00:34:45,892
The corrected model commits immediately; a controlled input instead waits for parent acceptance.

608
00:34:47,267 --> 00:34:48,623
SIG-002 · 1.

609
00:34:48,623 --> 00:34:51,336
Two sources of truth, synchronised once

610
00:34:52,462 --> 00:34:58,012
This is also non-breaking for consumers: the public names checked and checkedChange are unchanged.

611
00:34:59,387 --> 00:35:00,388
SIG-002 · 2.

612
00:35:00,388 --> 00:35:03,393
A rejected change leaves the switch out of sync

613
00:35:04,518 --> 00:35:07,952
This is the problem with the fixed version that strong candidates find.

614
00:35:07,952 --> 00:35:10,242
Suppose a consumer binds one way and decides

615
00:35:10,242 --> 00:35:12,531
in the handler whether to accept the change:

616
00:35:13,907 --> 00:35:17,662
SIG-002 · Answer · code 1/1

617
00:35:18,786 --> 00:35:24,892
The corrected model commits immediately; a controlled input instead waits for parent acceptance.

618
00:35:26,267 --> 00:35:27,347
SIG-002 · 2.

619
00:35:27,347 --> 00:35:30,589
A rejected change leaves the switch out of sync

620
00:35:31,715 --> 00:35:32,641
The user clicks.

621
00:35:32,641 --> 00:35:35,112
The model flips itself to true and emits.

622
00:35:35,112 --> 00:35:37,582
The parent refuses and leaves allowed as false.

623
00:35:37,582 --> 00:35:40,671
Angular only writes an input when the bound value changes,

624
00:35:40,671 --> 00:35:43,450
and allowed was false before and is still false,

625
00:35:43,450 --> 00:35:44,994
so nothing is written back.

626
00:35:44,994 --> 00:35:48,392
The switch shows "on" while the application believes it is "off".

627
00:35:49,767 --> 00:35:50,751
SIG-002 · 2.

628
00:35:50,751 --> 00:35:53,705
A rejected change leaves the switch out of sync

629
00:35:54,831 --> 00:35:56,519
There is no single right answer,

630
00:35:56,519 --> 00:35:58,770
but a library must pick one and document

631
00:35:58,770 --> 00:35:59,052
it:

632
00:36:00,427 --> 00:36:01,607
SIG-002 · 2.

633
00:36:01,607 --> 00:36:05,150
A rejected change leaves the switch out of sync

634
00:36:06,275 --> 00:36:09,076
Document ordinary two-way binding as an immediate-commit contract.

635
00:36:09,076 --> 00:36:13,627
A parent that must approve or reject a request needs a controlled contract.

636
00:36:13,627 --> 00:36:17,479
An immediate change-and-revert can leave the local model out of sync.

637
00:36:17,479 --> 00:36:21,330
Offer a separate request output (for example checkedChangeRequest) and a strictly

638
00:36:21,330 --> 00:36:24,832
controlled mode where the component never changes its own value.

639
00:36:26,207 --> 00:36:29,736
SIG-002 · 3. <ui-switch checked> does not work

640
00:36:30,861 --> 00:36:32,616
Consumers expect HTML-like boolean attributes.

641
00:36:32,616 --> 00:36:36,479
A plain input(false) would need the booleanAttribute transform for <ui-switch checked>

642
00:36:36,479 --> 00:36:39,990
to mean true, but model() does not accept a transform.

643
00:36:39,990 --> 00:36:44,555
With strict templates the static attribute is a type error (a string ''

644
00:36:44,555 --> 00:36:48,769
is not a boolean), which is at least caught at build time.

645
00:36:48,769 --> 00:36:49,472
Document [checked]="true".

646
00:36:50,847 --> 00:36:54,279
SIG-002 · What a strong candidate also mentions

647
00:36:55,404 --> 00:36:56,504
A disabled state.

648
00:36:56,504 --> 00:36:59,437
A switch with no disabled input forces consumers

649
00:36:59,437 --> 00:37:02,004
to wrap it or block clicks themselves.

650
00:37:02,004 --> 00:37:04,937
Use the native disabled attribute on the <button>,

651
00:37:04,937 --> 00:37:08,604
so it leaves the tab order and assistive technology reports

652
00:37:08,604 --> 00:37:08,971
it.

653
00:37:08,971 --> 00:37:09,337
Forms.

654
00:37:09,337 --> 00:37:13,371
Teams using reactive forms need a ControlValueAccessor as well (see API-004).

655
00:37:13,371 --> 00:37:18,504
The model and the form value must not become two sources of truth again.

656
00:37:18,504 --> 00:37:19,605
The accessible name.

657
00:37:19,605 --> 00:37:21,805
It comes from the projected content.

658
00:37:21,805 --> 00:37:24,738
A switch with no projected text needs an

659
00:37:24,738 --> 00:37:27,671
aria-label input, and the documentation must say so.

660
00:37:29,047 --> 00:37:31,581
SIG-002 · Strict parent authority

661
00:37:32,706 --> 00:37:36,442
A clarification about rollback: an immediate two-way accept-and-revert can leave

662
00:37:36,442 --> 00:37:40,178
the final parent value identical to the last bound value.

663
00:37:40,178 --> 00:37:42,794
The component model may therefore remain changed.

664
00:37:42,794 --> 00:37:46,157
Do not rely on synchronous rollback for strict authority.

665
00:37:46,157 --> 00:37:49,146
Use a read-only checked input and checkedChangeRequest output,

666
00:37:49,146 --> 00:37:52,509
with the template rendering only the accepted parent state.

667
00:37:52,509 --> 00:37:55,872
The separate controlled example demonstrates both acceptance and rejection.

668
00:37:57,247 --> 00:37:59,107
SIG-002 · Follow-up 1

669
00:38:00,232 --> 00:38:04,416
Would moving the copy into ngOnChanges have fixed the reset bug?

670
00:38:04,416 --> 00:38:06,319
What would still be wrong?

671
00:38:11,639 --> 00:38:15,049
It can fix the case where the parent actually changes the

672
00:38:15,049 --> 00:38:18,460
input, because each new bound value is copied into local state.

673
00:38:18,460 --> 00:38:21,250
It does not remove the two sources of truth.

674
00:38:21,250 --> 00:38:24,661
If the component changes its local state and the parent rejects

675
00:38:24,661 --> 00:38:26,831
the proposed value without changing its binding,

676
00:38:26,831 --> 00:38:30,242
there may be no input change and no lifecycle callback to

677
00:38:30,242 --> 00:38:31,482
undo the local state.

678
00:38:31,482 --> 00:38:34,273
You have also introduced an ordering rule between local

679
00:38:34,273 --> 00:38:37,063
updates and parent updates that must be maintained indefinitely.

680
00:38:37,063 --> 00:38:40,164
A model removes the duplicate storage for ordinary two-way binding.

681
00:38:40,164 --> 00:38:44,505
A controlled input and request output is clearer when the parent must approve changes.

682
00:38:44,505 --> 00:38:48,846
The right answer starts with ownership, not with replacing one lifecycle hook with another.

683
00:38:50,221 --> 00:38:52,149
SIG-002 · Follow-up 2

684
00:38:53,274 --> 00:38:57,526
How would you write a test that proves the rejected-change behavior you chose?

685
00:39:02,977 --> 00:39:06,893
Mount a parent host with checked false and an event handler that

686
00:39:06,893 --> 00:39:10,484
records the proposed true value but refuses to update accepted state.

687
00:39:10,484 --> 00:39:11,789
Click the actual button.

688
00:39:11,789 --> 00:39:15,053
Assert that the request was received, the parent remains false,

689
00:39:15,053 --> 00:39:18,644
and the rendered checked state also remains false under the controlled

690
00:39:18,644 --> 00:39:18,970
contract.

691
00:39:18,970 --> 00:39:21,908
Then change the handler to accept the next request

692
00:39:21,908 --> 00:39:24,845
and assert that both parent and view become true.

693
00:39:24,845 --> 00:39:28,435
Test the ordinary model contract separately: with an unchanged one-way parent

694
00:39:28,435 --> 00:39:31,699
binding, show that a local model change can remain true.

695
00:39:31,699 --> 00:39:35,290
Also exercise an immediate two-way accept-and-revert in the same event cycle,

696
00:39:35,290 --> 00:39:38,554
because the final parent value can equal its previous binding.

697
00:39:38,554 --> 00:39:42,144
That boundary explains why the lesson recommends an explicit request API

698
00:39:42,144 --> 00:39:45,734
for vetoable changes rather than relying on a rollback timing trick.

699
00:39:47,110 --> 00:39:49,345
SIG-002 · Follow-up 3

700
00:39:50,469 --> 00:39:53,997
When would you choose input plus output over model,

701
00:39:53,997 --> 00:39:57,525
even for a value the component can change itself?

702
00:40:02,692 --> 00:40:06,128
Choose input plus output when the component proposes changes

703
00:40:06,128 --> 00:40:09,183
but another owner decides whether to commit them.

704
00:40:09,183 --> 00:40:12,238
Examples include a server-authorized setting, a confirmation step,

705
00:40:12,238 --> 00:40:16,056
or a component whose value is part of a larger

706
00:40:16,056 --> 00:40:16,820
transactional form.

707
00:40:16,820 --> 00:40:21,784
The output can carry a request while the read-only input represents accepted state.

708
00:40:21,784 --> 00:40:24,456
This can also support an input transform,

709
00:40:24,456 --> 00:40:27,511
such as booleanAttribute, that model does not provide.

710
00:40:27,511 --> 00:40:31,330
Choose model when committing the local value immediately and emitting

711
00:40:31,330 --> 00:40:35,148
its change matches the intended contract, especially straightforward two-way binding.

712
00:40:35,148 --> 00:40:37,439
The tradeoff is convenience versus authority.

713
00:40:37,439 --> 00:40:41,257
Neither API automatically integrates every forms, validation, or accessibility requirement.

714
00:40:41,257 --> 00:40:44,694
State the contract with an example where the parent

715
00:40:44,694 --> 00:40:48,131
refuses a request; that example makes the difference concrete.

716
00:40:49,506 --> 00:40:51,627
SIG-003 · Scenario

717
00:40:52,752 --> 00:40:55,139
