00:44:30,536 --> 00:44:32,772
SIG-003 · Fixed version

771
00:44:33,897 --> 00:44:37,622
Closing on Escape now also returns focus to the anchor.

772
00:44:37,622 --> 00:44:41,721
Without that, keyboard users lose their place when the panel disappears.

773
00:44:43,096 --> 00:44:47,344
SIG-003 · What a strong candidate is still unhappy about

774
00:44:48,469 --> 00:44:50,594
Position only updates when signals change.

775
00:44:50,594 --> 00:44:54,135
Scrolling, resizing, or the anchor moving leaves the panel behind.

776
00:44:54,135 --> 00:44:59,093
A shared library should use a tested positioning engine, such as the CDK Overlay,

777
00:44:59,093 --> 00:45:04,050
or CSS anchor positioning where the supported browsers allow it, rather than hand-rolled coordinates.

778
00:45:04,050 --> 00:45:05,112
Escape is global.

779
00:45:05,112 --> 00:45:09,716
Every open popover closes on one Escape press, including ones underneath a dialog.

780
00:45:09,716 --> 00:45:12,194
Escape should close only the topmost layer.

781
00:45:12,194 --> 00:45:13,611
Edges of the viewport.

782
00:45:13,611 --> 00:45:17,152
There is no flipping when the panel would go off-screen.

783
00:45:17,152 --> 00:45:17,506
Semantics.

784
00:45:17,506 --> 00:45:20,693
The panel has no role, label or focus behaviour.

785
00:45:20,693 --> 00:45:24,234
Whether it is a dialog, a menu or a tooltip

786
00:45:24,234 --> 00:45:27,421
decides the ARIA pattern, and the component cannot guess.

787
00:45:28,796 --> 00:45:30,625
SIG-003 · Follow-up 1

788
00:45:31,750 --> 00:45:34,799
Why is once true on the listener not a fix?

789
00:45:40,181 --> 00:45:42,629
Once removes the listener after the first keydown

790
00:45:42,629 --> 00:45:44,772
event, not after the first Escape key.

791
00:45:44,772 --> 00:45:48,139
Pressing Tab or an arrow key can remove the handler while

792
00:45:48,139 --> 00:45:51,506
the popover is still open, so the next Escape does nothing.

793
00:45:51,506 --> 00:45:54,567
It also does not necessarily release a listener that never

794
00:45:54,567 --> 00:45:57,628
receives an event after the component closes or is destroyed.

795
00:45:57,628 --> 00:46:01,301
The intended lifetime is the period during which this layer is open.

796
00:46:01,301 --> 00:46:04,668
Register the handler when open becomes true and remove it through

797
00:46:04,668 --> 00:46:07,729
the effect cleanup when that run is replaced or destroyed.

798
00:46:07,729 --> 00:46:10,178
This test should include a non-Escape key before

799
00:46:10,178 --> 00:46:12,320
Escape and destruction without any key event.

800
00:46:12,320 --> 00:46:16,299
Those sequences expose why the browser option and the application lifecycle are different.

801
00:46:17,675 --> 00:46:19,795
SIG-003 · Follow-up 2

802
00:46:20,920 --> 00:46:25,304
When is a plain effect the right tool in a component library?

803
00:46:25,304 --> 00:46:26,400
Give an example.

804
00:46:31,745 --> 00:46:34,925
The open-state keyboard registration itself is a reasonable example.

805
00:46:34,925 --> 00:46:38,105
It connects reactive open state to a document event

806
00:46:38,105 --> 00:46:41,285
listener and does not depend on final element geometry.

807
00:46:41,285 --> 00:46:45,172
Another example is persisting a documented user preference to browser storage,

808
00:46:45,172 --> 00:46:49,059
provided the implementation handles browser availability, failures, and the correct lifetime.

809
00:46:49,059 --> 00:46:52,593
Avoid using an effect to copy one signal into another

810
00:46:52,593 --> 00:46:56,127
simply because you want the second to follow the first.

811
00:46:56,127 --> 00:46:58,247
Use a derivation for that relationship.

812
00:46:58,247 --> 00:47:01,074
An ordinary effect also should not be chosen

813
00:47:01,074 --> 00:47:03,901
for layout measurement merely because it eventually runs.

814
00:47:03,901 --> 00:47:06,374
Identify what timing the external operation requires.

815
00:47:06,374 --> 00:47:09,555
A listener can be installed without reading layout; measuring

816
00:47:09,555 --> 00:47:12,381
a rendered anchor requires a different integration point.

817
00:47:13,757 --> 00:47:15,881
SIG-003 · Follow-up 3

818
00:47:17,006 --> 00:47:20,250
How would you make Escape close only the topmost

819
00:47:20,250 --> 00:47:23,494
layer across popovers, menus, and dialogs from different teams?

820
00:47:28,900 --> 00:47:31,730
Introduce a shared layer coordinator or use the

821
00:47:31,730 --> 00:47:34,561
overlay infrastructure already provided by the component library.

822
00:47:34,561 --> 00:47:37,392
Each active layer registers a token, its ordering,

823
00:47:37,392 --> 00:47:39,869
an Escape handler, and the focus-restoration target.

824
00:47:39,869 --> 00:47:43,761
One shared keyboard dispatcher asks the topmost eligible layer to handle

825
00:47:43,761 --> 00:47:47,299
Escape, rather than every component independently subscribing and closing itself.

826
00:47:47,299 --> 00:47:50,484
Unregister a layer when it closes or is destroyed.

827
00:47:50,484 --> 00:47:53,314
Define what happens when a layer declines Escape,

828
00:47:53,314 --> 00:47:56,145
when a modal dialog is above a popover,

829
00:47:56,145 --> 00:47:58,976
and when the original focus target has disappeared.

830
00:47:58,976 --> 00:48:02,160
Test nested layers and verify that only the intended

831
00:48:02,160 --> 00:48:04,991
layer closes while the lower layer stays open.

832
00:48:04,991 --> 00:48:08,176
Keep the contract shared across teams; separate coordinators in

833
00:48:08,176 --> 00:48:11,007
each product package can recreate the global-listener problem.

834
