856
00:49:33,848 --> 00:49:35,596
There are at least four issues.

857
00:49:42,246 --> 00:49:45,352
SIG-004 · 1. sort() mutates the consumer's array

858
00:49:46,477 --> 00:49:49,480
Array.prototype.sort sorts in place and returns the same array.

859
00:49:49,480 --> 00:49:53,151
The computed therefore reorders the array the product team passed in,

860
00:49:53,151 --> 00:49:57,156
which is why their export and row numbers follow the table's sort

861
00:49:57,156 --> 00:49:57,490
order.

862
00:49:57,490 --> 00:50:00,159
A component must never modify data it receives

863
00:50:00,159 --> 00:50:02,496
through an input; the consumer owns it.

864
00:50:03,871 --> 00:50:06,893
SIG-004 · 1. sort() mutates the consumer's array

865
00:50:08,018 --> 00:50:11,857
There is a second effect: this.rows() returns the same reference after sorting,

866
00:50:11,857 --> 00:50:15,376
so if the consumer keeps that reference in its own signal,

867
00:50:15,376 --> 00:50:18,895
its own computeds see no change even though the contents moved.

868
00:50:20,271 --> 00:50:24,813
SIG-004 · 1. sort() mutates the consumer's array

869
00:50:25,938 --> 00:50:30,936
Fix: sort a copy, with toSorted (ES2023) or [...rows].sort(...).

870
00:50:32,311 --> 00:50:37,975
SIG-004 · 2. track $index ties DOM rows to positions, not records

871
00:50:39,100 --> 00:50:44,258
After sorting, row 0 holds a different record but Angular reuses the same <tr>.

872
00:50:44,258 --> 00:50:48,312
Any state inside a row (a focused checkbox, an expanded detail,

873
00:50:48,312 --> 00:50:53,102
a running animation) stays with the position and now belongs to the wrong

874
00:50:53,102 --> 00:50:53,471
record.

875
00:50:53,471 --> 00:50:57,155
It also means every row is re-bound on every sort.

876
00:50:58,531 --> 00:51:04,480
SIG-004 · 2. track $index ties DOM rows to positions, not records

877
00:51:05,605 --> 00:51:10,149
