878
00:51:10,149 --> 00:51:16,058
for example a trackBy input: trackBy = input<(row: T) => unknown>(row => row),

879
00:51:16,058 --> 00:51:17,876
and use track trackBy()(row).

880
00:51:19,251 --> 00:51:20,471
SIG-004 · 3.

881
00:51:20,471 --> 00:51:24,540
Expensive work on every new array, and one oversized computed

882
00:51:25,665 --> 00:51:29,383
The second report usually comes from a binding like [rows]="getOpenOrders()".

883
00:51:29,383 --> 00:51:33,473
That expression creates a new array on every change detection pass,

884
00:51:33,473 --> 00:51:36,448
so rows changes every time, the computed re-runs,

885
00:51:36,448 --> 00:51:38,679
and the whole table is re-sorted.

886
00:51:38,679 --> 00:51:41,282
With new row objects after every fetch,

887
00:51:41,282 --> 00:51:45,000
track $index (issue 2) cannot tell which rows are the

888
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:46,116
same records either.

889
00:51:47,491 --> 00:51:48,586
SIG-004 · 3.

890
00:51:48,586 --> 00:51:52,237
