891
00:51:53,362 --> 00:51:56,209
The table cannot stop a consumer from passing a

892
00:51:56,209 --> 00:51:59,056
new array, but it can avoid making it worse:

893
00:52:00,431 --> 00:52:01,662
SIG-004 · 3.

894
00:52:01,662 --> 00:52:05,769
Expensive work on every new array, and one oversized computed

895
00:52:06,893 --> 00:52:11,707
Keep each computed narrow. view bundles the rows and the count into a

896
00:52:11,707 --> 00:52:16,151
new object, so anything that reads view().count updates whenever the rows do.

897
00:52:16,151 --> 00:52:18,743
Separate sortedRows and rowCount computeds update independently.

898
00:52:18,743 --> 00:52:22,076
Use the equal option when a recomputation often produces

899
00:52:22,076 --> 00:52:25,038
an equivalent value that should not notify readers.

900
00:52:25,038 --> 00:52:29,852
Document the contract: pass a stable reference, ideally a signal or computed in

901
00:52:29,852 --> 00:52:34,296
the consumer, and provide a trackBy so re-fetched records keep their rows.

902
00:52:35,671 --> 00:52:36,965
SIG-004 · 4.

903
00:52:36,965 --> 00:52:38,691
Sorting is not accessible

904
00:52:39,817 --> 00:52:42,800
A click handler on <th> cannot be reached

905
00:52:42,800 --> 00:52:45,784
