924
00:54:05,931 --> 00:54:09,403
SIG-004 · Answer · code 5/5

925
00:54:10,527 --> 00:54:15,736
The correction preserves consumer data, tracks records, and provides accessible sorting controls.

926
00:54:17,111 --> 00:54:18,986
SIG-004 · Fixed version

927
00:54:20,111 --> 00:54:24,335
readonly T[] in the input type documents the contract ("the table will not

928
00:54:24,335 --> 00:54:28,236
change your array") and makes the compiler reject any future in-place sort.

929
00:54:29,611 --> 00:54:31,688
SIG-004 · Follow-up 1

930
00:54:32,813 --> 00:54:37,729
The consumer passes a new array on every fetch, but most records are identical.

931
00:54:37,729 --> 00:54:40,187
How would you avoid rebuilding every row?

932
00:54:45,601 --> 00:54:50,101
Track each row with a stable unique record identifier supplied by the consumer.

933
00:54:50,101 --> 00:54:53,564
Angular can then preserve the view associated with that record

934
00:54:53,564 --> 00:54:57,026
even if the array and record objects are newly allocated.

935
00:54:57,026 --> 00:55:01,527
Tracking by object identity alone loses that benefit when the fetch replaces objects;

936
00:55:01,527 --> 00:55:06,028
tracking by index can attach local state to the wrong record after sorting.

937
00:55:06,028 --> 00:55:09,490
Preserve references for unchanged records upstream if that is feasible,

938
00:55:09,490 --> 00:55:13,298
and use stable computed inputs to avoid unnecessary sorting on unrelated

939
00:55:13,298 --> 00:55:13,644
changes.

940
00:55:13,644 --> 00:55:17,799
Do not claim that tracking stops every render or every binding update.

941
00:55:17,799 --> 00:55:19,530
It chiefly establishes view identity.

942
00:55:19,530 --> 00:55:24,377
Measure sorting work, view creation, and binding updates separately, because they have different causes.

943
00:55:24,377 --> 00:55:27,147
Test with new objects containing the same identifiers

944
00:55:27,147 --> 00:55:29,571
and with genuinely added and removed records.

945
00:55:30,946 --> 00:55:32,926
SIG-004 · Follow-up 2

946
00:55:34,051 --> 00:55:37,458
Should the sort state be internal, a model, or both?

947
00:55:37,458 --> 00:55:41,547
What does each choice mean for teams that sort on the server?

948
00:55:46,828 --> 00:55:50,455
Internal sort state is convenient for an entirely client-side table: the

949
00:55:50,455 --> 00:55:53,753
component owns the interaction and derives the displayed rows locally.

950
00:55:53,753 --> 00:55:58,371
A model can expose that state for two-way use while retaining convenient local defaults.

951
00:55:58,371 --> 00:56:00,350
Server sorting needs a clearer boundary.

952
00:56:00,350 --> 00:56:03,977
A sort request should let the parent fetch a new page,

953
00:56:03,977 --> 00:56:08,595
and the table should not silently re-sort just that page as though it represented

954
00:56:08,595 --> 00:56:09,584
the full dataset.

955
00:56:09,584 --> 00:56:12,223
A controlled sort input plus request output supports

956
00:56:12,223 --> 00:56:14,531
server authority and rejected or pending requests.

957
00:56:14,531 --> 00:56:17,170
If both client and server modes are offered,

958
00:56:17,170 --> 00:56:21,127
make the mode explicit and document whether changing sort triggers local derivation

959
00:56:21,127 --> 00:56:22,117
or a request.

960
00:56:22,117 --> 00:56:25,085
Keep only one effective sort state within each mode.

961
00:56:25,085 --> 00:56:28,383
Test pending responses and stale fetches in the owning application

962
00:56:28,383 --> 00:56:31,352
rather than hiding network policy inside a generic table.

963
00:56:32,727 --> 00:56:34,703
SIG-004 · Follow-up 3

964
00:56:35,828 --> 00:56:40,532
How would you announce Sorted by name, ascending after a user activates a header?

965
00:56:45,960 --> 00:56:50,310
Keep a real button in the header so keyboard users can activate sorting,

966
00:56:50,310 --> 00:56:54,659
and expose the current direction with aria sort on the relevant header cell.

967
00:56:54,659 --> 00:56:59,343
If a supplemental announcement is needed, use a polite live region or the component

968
00:56:59,343 --> 00:57:03,693
library's LiveAnnouncer to report the accepted result, such as Sorted by name, ascending.

969
00:57:03,693 --> 00:57:06,035
Announce after the state has actually changed,

970
00:57:06,035 --> 00:57:08,377
not merely when a request was emitted,

971
00:57:08,377 --> 00:57:09,715
especially for server sorting.

972
00:57:09,715 --> 00:57:14,065
Avoid repeating a long message on every unrelated render or duplicating announcements unnecessarily.

973
00:57:14,065 --> 00:57:17,076
Maintain focus on the header button while rows reorder.

974
00:57:17,076 --> 00:57:20,422
Test the keyboard behavior and inspect the resulting accessibility attributes,

975
00:57:20,422 --> 00:57:24,102
then verify the announcement with the assistive technology supported by the

976
00:57:24,102 --> 00:57:24,437
project.

977
00:57:24,437 --> 00:57:28,451
A DOM assertion alone cannot prove what every screen reader will say.

978
00:57:29,827 --> 00:57:31,644
Final review checklist · 1

979
00:57:32,769 --> 00:57:37,606
You have now examined every main question and follow-up in the signals topic.

980
00:57:37,606 --> 00:57:41,699
The APIs are useful because they express different kinds of ownership.

981
00:57:41,699 --> 00:57:43,559
Signal stores a writable fact.

982
00:57:43,559 --> 00:57:45,419
Computed derives a read-only fact.

983
00:57:45,419 --> 00:57:49,512
Linked signal maintains writable state whose validity depends on another fact.

984
00:57:49,512 --> 00:57:53,233
Model exposes a locally writable input and its change output.

985
00:57:53,233 --> 00:57:56,954
A controlled input and request output keeps the parent authoritative.

986
00:57:56,954 --> 00:57:59,559
Effect crosses into an imperative external system.

987
00:57:59,559 --> 00:58:03,652
After render effect handles work that depends on rendered browser DOM.

988
00:58:05,027 --> 00:58:06,805
Final review checklist · 2

989
