When you answer these questions in an interview,

990
00:58:10,774 --> 00:58:13,619
lead with the observed defect and its mechanism.

991
00:58:13,619 --> 00:58:18,242
For the chip group, explain construction order, reference equality, and the feedback loop.

992
00:58:18,242 --> 00:58:21,087
For the switch, trace the two stored values

993
00:58:21,087 --> 00:58:23,576
and then discuss accepted versus requested changes.

994
00:58:23,576 --> 00:58:28,555
For the popover, identify the missing resource cleanup and the layout timing problem separately.

995
00:58:28,555 --> 00:58:31,399
For the table, explain input mutation, row identity,

996
00:58:31,399 --> 00:58:33,889
repeated computation, keyboard access, and comparison semantics.

997
00:58:33,889 --> 00:58:38,511
Then name a test that would fail before your correction and pass afterward.

998
00:58:39,887 --> 00:58:41,671
Final review checklist · 3

999
00:58:42,796 --> 00:58:47,078
Finally, treat a shared component library as a contract with other teams.

1000
00:58:47,078 --> 00:58:49,933
Document whether outputs represent interactions or effective state,

1001
00:58:49,933 --> 00:58:53,145
what boolean syntax is accepted, what identifies a row,

1002
00:58:53,145 --> 00:58:55,286
and what happens when options disappear.

1003
00:58:55,286 --> 00:58:59,569
Keep minimal instructional fixes distinct from production overlay, forms, and accessibility requirements.

1004
00:58:59,569 --> 00:59:02,424
The best answer is specific enough to implement

1005
00:59:02,424 --> 00:59:04,922
and honest enough to state its limits.

1006
00:59:04,922 --> 00:59:08,491
Use the transcript and code examples to revisit any section,

1007
00:59:08,491 --> 00:59:12,060
and practice explaining the diagnosis without relying on the slide.

1008
00:59:12,060 --> 00:59:15,629
That is the skill these scenarios are designed to develop.

1009
00:59:16,670 --> 00:59:18,812
That's the end of lesson 1.

